(defxdoc+ lexer
  :parents (syntax-for-tools)
  :short "An executable lexer for Rust."
  :long
  (xdoc::topstring
   (xdoc::p
    "The lexer turns a sequence of Unicode scalar values
     (natural numbers; UTF-8 decoding happens elsewhere)
     into a sequence of lexeme concrete syntax trees (CSTs)
     of the ABNF lexical grammar (see @(see grammar)).
     The lexing functions follow the @(tsee fty::defresult) style
     of the ABNF library's "
    (xdoc::seetopic "abnf::parsing-primitives-defresult"
                    "parsing primitives")
    ": each takes a list of natural numbers as input,
     and returns a CST (or list of CSTs) or an error,
     along with the remaining input.")
   (xdoc::p
    "There is one lexing function per grammar rule,
     plus functions for repetitions and anonymous groups,
     and dispatch functions that choose alternatives by
     looking ahead a few characters.
     The extra-grammatical side conditions
     (see @(see extra-grammatical-restrictions))
     are implemented at their points of relevance:
     [SC2] block comment closing is inherent in
     the recursive structure of the block comment lexing functions;
     [SC7] makes integer literal lexing commit to
     a radix after @('0b'), @('0o'), or @('0x');
     [SC8] rejects an identifier, or a lone underscore,
     immediately followed by @('#'), a single quote, or a double quote;
     [SC9] rejects unicode escapes that do not denote
     Unicode scalar values (or that denote NUL, in C strings);
     [SC10] ends a raw literal at its first terminator
     and bounds the number of @('#') delimiting it;
     and [SC11] rejects a character or string literal
     immediately followed by an underscore.
     Maximal munch [SC1] is implemented by
     greedy repetitions and longest-first dispatch.")
   (xdoc::p
    "Lexing is edition-independent:
     the grammar has a single @('identifier-or-keyword') rule,
     and the edition-dependent classification of keywords [SC6]
     happens in the tokenizer, which maps lexeme CSTs to tokens.")
   (xdoc::p
    "Each lexing function comes with several kinds of theorems:
     linear rules bounding the length of the remaining input;
     input decomposition theorems, saying that
     the string at the fringe of the returned CST
     followed by the remaining input
     is the original input;
     and grammar soundness theorems, saying that
     the returned CST is terminated and matches the grammar rule
     (stated via the @('cst-matchp') predicates
     generated by @(tsee abnf::deftreeops),
     with separate terminatedness theorems
     in the form needed to compose the proofs).
     The deeper properties that lexing results satisfy
     the declarative specification @(tsee lexing-okp),
     including maximal munch [SC1],
     are future work.")
   (xdoc::p
    "The lexer aims at being clearly correct rather than fast;
     a faster lexer (e.g. using a stobj for the input)
     can later be proved equivalent to this one."))
  :order-subtopics t
  :default-parent t)

